WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.623 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Imagine two neurons whose activity settles to a steady
pattern.

2
00:00:04.623 --> 00:00:10.861 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Give the pattern a small nudge. If it returns, the model can
hold that pattern like a

3
00:00:10.861 --> 00:00:15.264 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
memory. Can you predict these memories just from the wiring?

4
00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:19.575 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A firing rate is a nonnegative number describing activity.

5
00:00:19.575 --> 00:00:24.537 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We are studying a mathematical network model, not measuring
a real brain.

6
00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:30.246 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its rate changes by balancing natural decay against input
from the other neurons and

7
00:00:30.246 --> 00:00:31.401 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
a constant drive.

8
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.524 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The threshold is simple. If the total input is positive,
keep it.

9
00:00:36.524 --> 00:00:39.286 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
If it is negative, replace it by zero.

10
00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:44.664 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Draw the output against input: flat on the left, then rising
on the right.

11
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:50.889 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At the settings used here, an arrow means weaker inhibition,
with weight minus three

12
00:00:50.889 --> 00:00:55.142 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
quarters. Without an arrow, the weight is minus three
halves.

13
00:00:55.142 --> 00:01:01.137 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Both interactions suppress activity. An arrow is not a
positive excitatory connection.

14
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.322 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Take two neurons with arrows both ways.

15
00:01:04.322 --> 00:01:09.613 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
If both fire at rate x, each receives input one minus three
quarters of x.

16
00:01:09.613 --> 00:01:15.189 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At equilibrium, the firing rate equals that input, so the
rate stops changing.

17
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:18.321 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Move the three quarters of x to the left.

18
00:01:18.321 --> 00:01:20.940 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
One and three quarters of x equals one.

19
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.560 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Multiply by four, then divide by seven.

20
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.583 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each neuron settles at exactly four sevenths.

21
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:31.697 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Now raise the first rate a little. It suppresses the second
more strongly.

22
00:01:31.697 --> 00:01:35.086 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
That drop then releases some inhibition on the first.

23
00:01:35.086 --> 00:01:40.520 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This is positive feedback through two negative connections,
even though neither arrow

24
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.415 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
is excitatory.

25
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.464 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
But feedback does not automatically win.

26
00:01:44.464 --> 00:01:48.661 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here the natural decay is strong enough to damp the
difference.

27
00:01:48.661 --> 00:01:52.857 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
One rate falls while the other rises back toward four
sevenths.

28
00:01:52.857 --> 00:01:56.920 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Returning after sufficiently small nudges is local
stability.

29
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.892 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The active neurons are called the support.

30
00:01:59.892 --> 00:02:04.212 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A clique is a group with arrows in both directions between
every pair.

31
00:02:04.212 --> 00:02:08.964 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Our two-neuron example is a clique. Here is the same idea
with three neurons.

32
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:13.714 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A target is an outside neuron receiving arrows from the
whole group.

33
00:02:13.714 --> 00:02:18.416 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Remove one of those arrows and that outside neuron is no
longer a target.

34
00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:22.989 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A clique is target-free when no outside neuron receives from
all of it.

35
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:28.699 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Curto, Geneson and Morrison conjectured that only
target-free cliques could support stable

36
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:32.254 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
memories. The direction saying these cliques work is proved.

37
00:02:32.254 --> 00:02:37.527 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The converse is the vulnerable part: could a stable pattern
use a group missing an arrow?

38
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:44.259 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We will examine an eleven-neuron example at the standard
settings: epsilon one quarter,

39
00:02:44.259 --> 00:02:50.692 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
delta one half, and drive one. All eleven neurons stay
active and stable, although their

40
00:02:50.692 --> 00:02:52.300 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
group is not a clique.

41
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.392 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Start with every possible arrow between different neurons.

42
00:02:56.392 --> 00:03:00.210 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
There are a hundred and ten. Delete these twenty-one arrows.

43
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:05.557 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The red dashed arrows show absences. Every connection still
acts; the missing arrows

44
00:03:05.557 --> 00:03:07.148 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
mean stronger inhibition.

45
00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:13.413 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here is the actual equilibrium. The bars show exact
numerators, all divided by two hundred

46
00:03:13.413 --> 00:03:19.227 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
and ninety-two. Every bar is positive, so the support really
contains all eleven neurons.

47
00:03:19.227 --> 00:03:22.101 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The unequal heights are part of the pattern.

48
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:28.148 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Check neuron zero. Its stronger inhibitors are neurons five
and eight, with numerators

49
00:03:28.148 --> 00:03:34.060 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
thirty-seven and ten. The other ten rates have total
numerator three hundred and thirteen.

50
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.708 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Start with weak inhibition from all ten, then add the extra
inhibition from those two.

51
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:45.680 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The input becomes one minus three quarters times three
hundred and sixty over two hundred

52
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.071 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
and ninety-two. That equals twenty-two over two hundred and
ninety-two, exactly neuron

53
00:03:51.071 --> 00:03:55.460 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
zero's rate. The certificate verifies the same balance for
all eleven.

54
00:03:55.833 --> 00:04:00.659 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A matrix is just a rectangular table that records a linear
calculation.

55
00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.620 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For this network, collect the equilibrium equations and
multiply by four.

56
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:11.193 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The diagonal entries become four. An arrow gives three; a
missing arrow gives six.

57
00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:17.299 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Reading one row means multiplying its entries by the
corresponding rates and adding.

58
00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:21.667 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The whole table times the rate list must give four in every
row.

59
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:25.967 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Our exact rate list passes this test, with no decimal
rounding.

60
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:32.902 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Balance alone is not enough. A pencil balanced on its tip is
at rest, but a nudge grows.

61
00:04:32.902 --> 00:04:36.485 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here we examine deviations from the equilibrium.

62
00:04:36.485 --> 00:04:43.054 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Since every rate stays positive nearby, the threshold does
not interfere with this local

63
00:04:43.054 --> 00:04:43.949 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
calculation.

64
00:04:44.333 --> 00:04:49.853 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For one changing number, a negative multiplier in its
rate-of-change equation pulls it

65
00:04:49.853 --> 00:04:54.218 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
back toward zero. In several dimensions a deviation can also
rotate.

66
00:04:54.218 --> 00:04:58.133 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
What matters is whether the rotating motion shrinks or
grows.

67
00:04:58.533 --> 00:05:01.999 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Eigenvalues describe these basic modes of change.

68
00:05:01.999 --> 00:05:05.817 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A complex pair combines rotation with growth or decay.

69
00:05:05.817 --> 00:05:11.970 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its real part controls the envelope. Here the deviation
equation uses minus one quarter

70
00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.101 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
of the matrix K.

71
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.892 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
So we need every eigenvalue of K to lie to the right of
zero.

72
00:05:17.892 --> 00:05:24.084 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Numerically they do. The closest real part is about zero
point zero three seven seven.

73
00:05:24.084 --> 00:05:28.548 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
That thin margin warns us that a picture alone is not a
proof.

74
00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:31.992 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The exact test works with a polynomial instead.

75
00:05:31.992 --> 00:05:37.785 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Even for a quadratic, coefficients can tell you whether all
roots lie on the stable side.

76
00:05:37.785 --> 00:05:43.317 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The Hurwitz criterion generalises this idea using
determinants, precise numbers built

77
00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:44.749 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
from the coefficients.

78
00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:50.744 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For this degree-eleven polynomial, all eleven required
Hurwitz determinants are positive.

79
00:05:50.744 --> 00:05:56.355 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The first three are forty-four, seven thousand five hundred
and fourteen, and one million

80
00:05:56.355 --> 00:06:00.327 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
eight hundred and sixty-seven thousand nine hundred and
twenty.

81
00:06:00.327 --> 00:06:03.037 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Exact arithmetic certifies local stability.

82
00:06:03.433 --> 00:06:09.292 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
One more technical issue matters. The conjecture assumes
certain equation systems have

83
00:06:09.292 --> 00:06:15.354 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
no accidental zero determinants. A determinant tells us
whether a linear system collapses

84
00:06:15.354 --> 00:06:19.441 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
a direction. Nonzero means its square table can be inverted.

85
00:06:19.833 --> 00:06:23.295 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At these settings there is a neat whole-number argument.

86
00:06:23.295 --> 00:06:26.262 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Look only at remainders after division by three.

87
00:06:26.262 --> 00:06:29.476 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Four leaves remainder one. Three and six leave zero.

88
00:06:29.476 --> 00:06:32.937 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
So every such network matrix becomes the identity table.

89
00:06:33.333 --> 00:06:39.451 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The identity has determinant one. Therefore each principal
determinant of the original

90
00:06:39.451 --> 00:06:43.363 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
integer table has remainder one, and cannot equal zero.

91
00:06:43.363 --> 00:06:47.916 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For the other required determinants, replace one column by
ones.

92
00:06:47.916 --> 00:06:51.045 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The same remainder argument still gives one.

93
00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:56.864 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This works for every graph at the standard parameters, not
just eleven neurons.

94
00:06:56.864 --> 00:06:59.546 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It proves nondegeneracy, not stability.

95
00:06:59.546 --> 00:07:05.320 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Stability still needs the positive equilibrium and the
separate exact Hurwitz checks

96
00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:06.145 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
we just saw.

97
00:07:06.533 --> 00:07:13.312 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
How small can a counterexample be? For this particular
graph, every proper induced nonclique

98
00:07:13.312 --> 00:07:19.207 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
subgraph fails. That means keep a subset of neurons and all
arrows between them.

99
00:07:19.207 --> 00:07:23.333 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It does not mean every smaller graph in the world fails.

100
00:07:23.733 --> 00:07:29.603 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A separate exhaustive enumeration covers all seven-neuron
directed graph shapes, over

101
00:07:29.603 --> 00:07:33.540 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
eight hundred and eighty-two million, plus smaller sizes.

102
00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.062 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Certified rejection bounds remove impossible cases.

103
00:07:37.062 --> 00:07:43.070 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
All thirty-five thousand eight hundred and forty-two saved
seven-neuron candidates fail

104
00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.589 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
exact stability tests.

105
00:07:44.967 --> 00:07:51.183 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The result brackets the smallest possible nonclique support
between eight and eleven

106
00:07:51.183 --> 00:07:55.328 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
neurons. Eight, nine and ten remain unresolved globally.

107
00:07:55.328 --> 00:08:01.767 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Eleven is minimal under vertex deletion, but it is not
proved to be the global minimum.

108
00:08:02.167 --> 00:08:07.232 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A numerical trajectory shows one small perturbation
returning to the actual equilibrium.

109
00:08:07.232 --> 00:08:11.261 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This illustrates the behaviour, while the algebra proves
local return.

110
00:08:11.261 --> 00:08:16.269 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It does not show that every starting state returns, or
certify a fixed percentage-sized

111
00:08:16.269 --> 00:08:16.615 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
basin.

112
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.316 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This is not the first counterexample. Geneson disproved the
general conjecture with six

113
00:08:22.316 --> 00:08:27.509 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
neurons at different settings. The public repository
hturcotte1 slash C L T N already

114
00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:33.008 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
implied counterexamples at the standard settings, including
one with twenty-three neurons.

115
00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:38.324 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Those repository results were present in commit three seven
four four two zero zero,

116
00:08:38.324 --> 00:08:41.255 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
dated the fifteenth of October, twenty twenty-six.

117
00:08:41.255 --> 00:08:46.473 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Our eleven-neuron graph came from shrinking its
twenty-three-neuron example and modifying

118
00:08:46.473 --> 00:08:50.752 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
arrows. Its parameter map also already implied the
small-order exclusion.

119
00:08:51.133 --> 00:08:57.432 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The retained contribution is this smaller explicit witness,
exact checks of its vertex-minimality,

120
00:08:57.432 --> 00:09:02.446 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
the universal remainder argument, and an independently
certified size bracket.

121
00:09:02.446 --> 00:09:08.166 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Remember: equilibrium means balanced rates; stability means
local return; the full active

122
00:09:08.166 --> 00:09:09.901 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
group need not be a clique.

123
00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:16.006 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The companion paper and certificates provide the complete
graph, rational rates and stability

124
00:09:16.006 --> 00:09:20.239 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
calculations. This film develops their mechanism and proof
structure.

125
00:09:20.239 --> 00:09:25.393 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The exact minimum at the standard parameters is still an
open question between eight

126
00:09:25.393 --> 00:09:26.068 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
and eleven.
